iance of the response variable should be constant across all levels of the factors.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nThis factorial design is applicable when the researcher aims to explore multiple factors simultaneously and their interactions. It is particularly useful when the factors are expected to have both main effects and interaction effects on the response variable. Selection criteria include the need for efficiency in experimentation and the ability to analyze complex interactions.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of the data collected from this factorial experiment typically involves Analysis of Variance (ANOVA). The ANOVA will help determine whether the main effects and interaction effects are statistically significant. Post-hoc tests may be conducted if significant differences are found.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nThe strengths of using a factorial design include:\n- The ability to evaluate multiple factors and their interactions in a single experiment.\n- Increased efficiency in resource utilization compared to conducting separate experiments for each factor.\n- Comprehensive insights into the system being studied, leading to better decision-making.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nSome limitations of factorial designs include:\n- The complexity of analysis increases with the number of factors and levels, which can lead to difficulties in interpretation.\n- If the number of factors is large, the number of experimental runs can become impractically high (e.g., a full factorial design with many factors may require an extensive number of runs).\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated designs include fractional factorial designs, which allow for the study of a subset of factor combinations, and response surface methodology, which is used for optimizing processes. Alternatives may include one-factor-at-a-time experiments, though these are less efficient for exploring interactions.\n\n**Practica